or the Intervals login URL with a fetch call (a POST that includes the token) and then sends the browser there.\n2. **Intervals rejected the request with 400 `invalid_request`.** The permission list asked for read and write on the same area (e.g. `CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`). On Intervals, write already includes read, and naming an area twice gets the request rejected. That's why pasting the link stopped working once calendar sync shipped. The list is now `ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE`.\n3. **Rivera was told to reconnect after an hour.** Intervals tokens don't expire until the athlete revokes access, and there's no refresh token. The code assumed a one-hour lifetime, then threw the token away when it couldn't refresh it. Now a token with no stated expiry is kept, and the connection is only marked \"expired, reconnect\" when Intervals actually returns 401.\n\n**Testing:** I ran the whole connect flow against a fake Intervals server I wrote, and it works end to end. Connecting succeeds, wellness data loads, and a token with no expiry stays connected through the background refresh. I couldn't test against the real intervals.icu.\n\n**Port conflict:** a `server.py` you already had running (PID 93026) holds port 8766. I left it alone and tested a copy on port 8767 instead. Restart yours to pick up the changes.\n\n**After deploying:** Rivera needs to click Connect once more, because the old code already deleted their token. Everything is uncommitted.","ttft_ms":3476,"type":"result","duration_ms":119030,"uuid":"3866e878-dd55-4fef-ae65-17d8dc8f14f4","ttft_stream_ms":2556,"time_to_request_ms":886,"first_content_frame_ms":2557,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
